+## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch, JsonMergePatchError } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyMergePatch(
+  { title: "Goodbye!", author: { givenName: "John", familyName: "Doe" }, tags: ["a", "b"] },
+  { title: "Hello!", author: { familyName: null }, tags: ["a"] },
+); // { title: "Hello!", author: { givenName: "John" }, tags: ["a"] }
+```
+
+- `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` merges `patch` into `target` and returns the result as a new value.
+  It never modifies `target` or `patch`, and the result shares no object with them.
+- An object patch changes only the members it names, recursively: a member set to `null` is removed
+  (removing a missing member is not an error), any other value is merged into the target's member. A
+  target that is not an object is first replaced by `{}`. A patch that is not an object (an array, a
+  string, a number, a boolean or `null`) replaces the whole target: arrays are never merged, and a
+  `null` inside an array is kept.
+- As RFC 7386 notes, a merge patch cannot set a member to `null`, nor change part of an array: use
+  `applyPatch` for those.
+- It throws a `JsonMergePatchError` when `target` or `patch` is not a JSON value, by the same rules as
+  `applyPatch` (`undefined`, a function, `NaN`, a `Date`, a sparse array, a cyclic structure, a structure
+  too deep for the call stack...), even in a part of the target the patch replaces. Its `cause` is the
+  underlying error, and no other error escapes, even one raised by a getter.
+- Only own members are merged into; a member named `__proto__` is an ordinary member and never changes
+  a prototype.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
