+---
+
+# JSON Merge Patch: copy-on-apply, strict JSON inputs, JsonMergePatchError
+
+## Context
+
+Clients send partial updates as JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386); `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` (task-004)
+implements it. The RFC's section 2 algorithm assigns into the target in place, and says nothing on
+inputs that are not JSON, nor on JavaScript's inherited properties (`__proto__`, `constructor`). The
+library already makes `applyPatch` strict about JSON values and side-effect free (dl-003 and dl-004,
+both still pending); merge patch payloads come from clients, so they are untrusted input.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Follow RFC 7386 section 2 exactly: an object patch merges member by member, `null` removes, a
+  non-object target becomes `{}`; any other patch (arrays included) replaces the target.
+- Copy-on-apply: deep-copy `target` and `patch`, then merge the copies; the inputs are never modified
+  and the result shares no object with them.
+- Both inputs must be JSON values, checked by the same rules as `applyPatch`. The validation and copy
+  helpers (`cloneJson`, `isPlainObject`, `setMember`) move from `json-patch.ts` to an internal module,
+  `json-value.ts`, shared by both features; `applyPatch` is unchanged.
+- The whole target is validated, even the parts the patch replaces or removes: a target that is not JSON
+  is an error at the boundary, whatever the patch.
+- Every failure throws a `JsonMergePatchError` (new export) whose `cause` is the underlying error; no
+  other error escapes, even one raised by a getter or a stack overflow.
+- Only own members of the target are merged into; members are written as own data properties, so
+  `__proto__` is an ordinary member.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers get a fresh document and can keep using their inputs; the cost is one copy of each input.
+- JSON Patch and JSON Merge Patch accept exactly the same values, from one implementation.
+- `JsonMergePatchError` has no `index` or `operation`: a merge patch has no operations to point to.
+- As the RFC says, a merge patch cannot set `null` or change part of an array; the README points to
+  `applyPatch` for those.
